    <!-- ===================================================
             PHASE 4 ADVANTAGES
             ===================================================== -->
    <hr class="p4-sep" />

    <div class="p4-section-label">Design Advantages</div>
    <h2 class="p4-section-title">Rail-driven inversion · Clean power transfer</h2>

    <div class="p4-phase-grid">
        <div class="p4-phase-card" data-num="1">
            <h3>Stationary Pistons</h3>
            <p>Pistons don't move—they sit fixed in position. No piston-ring wear, no friction losses from piston travel. Rail does all the work.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-phase-card" data-num="2">
            <h3>No Sealed Chambers</h3>
            <p>Pistons exposed to atmosphere along the rail path. Zero oil accumulation, zero water condensation, zero corrosion inside sealed bores.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-phase-card" data-num="3">
            <h3>Unified Transmission Coupling</h3>
            <p>All pistons feed into one transmission block. Synchronized power delivery; no individual piston timing issues or imbalance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-phase-card" data-num="4">
            <h3>Simplified Rail Propulsion</h3>
            <p>Single moving rail mass is lighter than four moving pistons. Pressure system only needs to accelerate one component repeatedly.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ===================================================
             PHASE 4 MECHANICAL FLOW
             ===================================================== -->
    <div class="p4-section-label">Operating Cycle</div>
    <h2 class="p4-section-title">Phase 4 — Step-by-step piston push sequence</h2>

    <div class="p4-phase-grid">
        <div class="p4-phase-card" data-num="A">
            <h3>Step 1: Rail Acceleration</h3>
            <p>Pressure valve opens. Steam accelerates the moving rail forward (left to right) along its guide rails.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-phase-card" data-num="B">
            <h3>Step 2: Piston Push</h3>
            <p>As rail moves forward, its leading edge contacts and pushes all stationary pistons simultaneously into the transmission coupling.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-phase-card" data-num="C">
            <h3>Step 3: Transmission Conversion</h3>
            <p>Transmission block receives linear push force from all pistons. Internal gearing converts linear impulse to rotational wheel drive.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-phase-card" data-num="D">
            <h3>Step 4: Rail Return</h3>
            <p>Pressure valve closes. Spring return or reverse pressure retracts the rail. Pistons reset to neutral. Cycle repeats at set frequency.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ===================================================
             PHASE 4 DESIGN CONSIDERATIONS
             ===================================================== -->
    <div class="p4-section-label">Design Considerations</div>
    <h2 class="p4-section-title">Phase 4 trade-offs and technical constraints</h2>

    <div class="p4-flaw-grid">
        <div class="p4-flaw major">
            <div class="p4-flaw-tag">Major Consideration</div>
            <h4>Rail Guide Precision</h4>
            <p>Moving rail must maintain perfect alignment across its entire travel distance. Any binding or misalignment causes uneven piston push.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-flaw thermal">
            <div class="p4-flaw-tag">Thermal Challenge</div>
            <h4>Reciprocation Speed Limits</h4>
            <p>Pressure system must achieve high reciprocation frequency (100+ cycles/min) to maintain continuous wheel rotation. Pressure demand increases with speed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-flaw design">
            <div class="p4-flaw-tag">Design Constraint</div>
            <h4>Piston Synchronization</h4>
            <p>All pistons must contact transmission simultaneously. If piston heights vary, force distribution becomes unequal and transmission coupling imbalances.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p4-flaw critical">
            <div class="p4-flaw-tag">Critical Requirement</div>
            <h4>Return Mechanism Reliability</h4>
            <p>Rail must reliably reset for each cycle. Failed return spring or reverse-pressure system halts all motion. Redundancy or predictive monitoring essential.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>

